if (unlikely(irqd_irq_disabled(d))) {
    plic_toggle(handler, d->hwirq, 1);
Reason:
`irqd_irq_disabled(d)` only checks the global interrupt disabled state, but after an affinity change the PLIC enable bit for the handling hart can be cleared even when the interrupt is not globally disabled. Thus the subsequent completion (`writel`) is silently ignored by PLIC because the interrupt is disabled on that hart, causing the interrupt to never be completed and the device to freeze.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
reg = handler->enable_base + (d->hwirq / 32) * sizeof(u32);
enabled = readl(reg) & BIT(d->hwirq % 32);
if (unlikely(!enabled)) {
Reason:
The fix reads the actual per-hart PLIC enable register to determine if the interrupt is enabled on the current hart. If it is not enabled (even if globally enabled), the code temporarily re-enables it before sending the completion, ensuring PLIC processes the completion message. This correctly handles the case where affinity change has cleared the enable bit for the handling hart.

